<h1>Hello World test structure</h1>
<p>Assuming you read the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/">Orcraft tutorial overview</a>, we can start developing a first test. This is a minimal implementation of an Orcraft test.</p>
<p>Open the following file in your preferred IDE (we use VS Code for this demonstration) and let's review its code:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_intro/test_hello_world/test_hello_world.py") }}

<h2 id="imports">Imports</h2>
{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftLogger as Logger,
	OrcraftTest,
	OrcraftTestConfig,
	OrcraftTestCase,
	OrcraftConfigField,
)') }}
<p><code>from __future__ import annotations</code> lets the type hints below (e.g. <code>TestHelloWorldConfig | None</code>)
  use the modern union syntax regardless of the Python version running the test.</p>
<p>The test imports the building blocks it needs from the <code>orcraft</code> package: a logger, the base test and config classes, the test case type, and the config field helpers.</p>

<h2 id="config-class">Config class</h2>
{{ codeblock.block('class TestHelloWorldConfig(OrcraftTestConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="Hello World")
	
	description = OrcraftConfigField.Text(
		default=(
			"This is a minimal implementation of an Orcraft Test.\\n"
			"It demonstrates how to define a test config, inherit from OrcraftTest, and implement a single test case.\\n"
			"When executed, the test logs a simple Hello World message and returns a passing test result."
		)
	)') }}
<p><code>TestHelloWorldConfig</code> is this test's user-defined config, inheriting from the framework's
  <code>OrcraftTestConfig</code>. Its fields (<code>name</code> and
  <code>description</code>) drive what is shown for this test in the controller's GUI and in the CLI's run tables.</p>
<p><code>name</code> and <code>description</code> are both built into <code>OrcraftTestConfig</code> itself, not
  something this test adds - every test config exposes them. Overriding <code>default</code> here only replaces
  the value shown when no other value is configured; it does not change what the field is used for.</p>
<p>Some fields are editable from the controller's GUI, while others are not - <code>name</code> and
  <code>description</code> are examples of fields that aren't configurable there. Complete documentation of
  config fields is available in the <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/">Framework</a> page.</p>

<h2 id="test-class">Test class</h2>
{{ codeblock.block('class TestHelloWorld(OrcraftTest):
	def __init__(self, config: TestHelloWorldConfig | None = None) -> None:
		super().__init__(config or TestHelloWorldConfig())

	def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

		Logger.log("Hello World", color=Logger.Color.BLUE)

		return case.result') }}
<p><code>TestHelloWorld</code> is the test itself. It accepts an optional config in <code>__init__</code>, falling
  back to the default, and implements <code>_run_case</code>, which is invoked once per configured test case.</p>

<h2 id="main">Main</h2>
{{ codeblock.block('def main() -> None:
	test = TestHelloWorld()
	test.run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}
<p>Finally, <code>main</code> lets the test run standalone, outside the controller, when the file is executed
  directly as a script.</p>
